From: JiaY-shi <shi05275@163.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 14:30:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath12k: quarantine unconfirmed ML peer IDs

An ML peer ID is safe to reuse only after every link peer has been
deleted from firmware.  ath12k currently discards the result of the MLO
peer delete operation and unconditionally releases a host-allocated ID.
It also loses cleanup failures while rolling back peer creation.  A late
or missing peer unmap can therefore allow a new station to reuse an ID
which firmware still owns, matching the duplicate-ID firmware failure
described by Qualcomm's peer assoc status fix.

Keep a quarantine bitmap in addition to the live allocation bitmap.
When firmware cleanup cannot be confirmed outside recovery, reserve the
host-allocated ML peer ID until firmware restarts.  A peer-assoc rejection
is itself proof that firmware still owns the ID, so quarantine it
immediately even if deleting the newly-created link peer later succeeds.
Continue tearing down host state so mac80211 can remove the station, but
never hand that ID to another client while the old firmware instance is
running.  Platforms where firmware allocates the ML peer ID are left
unchanged.

Also make peer-create rollback cover create-map timeouts, missing DP peer
state, DP assignment failures, and later station setup failures.  Missing
link state during a full MLO delete is treated as an unconfirmed delete
instead of silent success.

During SSR, normal peer cleanup releases live allocations and the restarted
firmware has an empty peer table, so release quarantined IDs as well.

This trades one ID after an exceptional cleanup failure for avoiding a
whole-WCSS firmware crash.  The loss remains bounded until recovery and is
reported with an explicit quarantine warning.

Signed-off-by: JiaY-shi <shi05275@163.com>
